AHS-014 — Decision Architecture

Document ID: AHS-014
Title: Decision Architecture
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

Architectural Views: Logical, Process, Information

1. Purpose

This specification defines the canonical Decision Architecture of the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

Decision Architecture governs how engineering cognition is transformed into explainable, traceable, evidence-based engineering decisions and recommendations while preserving human oversight and engineering governance.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Decision model
Decision lifecycle
Decision inputs
Decision outputs
Decision quality
Decision governance
Decision validation

This specification does not define optimization algorithms or domain-specific decision policies.

3. Canonical Definition

An Engineering Decision is a justified selection among one or more engineering alternatives based on validated evidence, engineering understanding, knowledge, reasoning, constraints, and intended objectives.

4. Decision Objectives

Atlas SHALL produce decisions that are:

Evidence-based
Explainable
Traceable
Context-aware
Constraint-compliant
Confidence-qualified
Human-reviewable
5. Decision Inputs

Every decision SHALL be based upon the following canonical inputs:

Engineering Context
Engineering Intent
Engineering Constraints
Engineering Evidence
Engineering Understanding
Engineering Knowledge
Engineering Conclusions
Applicable Policies
Confidence Assessment

No mandatory input SHALL be omitted unless explicitly justified.

6. Decision Lifecycle
Decision Request
        │
        ▼
Decision Formulation
        │
        ▼
Alternative Generation
        │
        ▼
Alternative Evaluation
        │
        ▼
Decision Selection
        │
        ▼
Decision Validation
        │
        ▼
Decision Approval
        │
        ▼
Decision Communication
        │
        ▼
Decision Monitoring

The lifecycle SHALL be fully traceable.

7. Alternative Generation

Atlas SHALL identify feasible engineering alternatives where applicable.

Alternatives MAY originate from:

Engineering reasoning
Historical engineering knowledge
Human expertise
Organizational standards
Optimization processes

Each alternative SHALL include sufficient information for evaluation.

8. Alternative Evaluation

Alternatives SHALL be evaluated against:

Engineering objectives
Applicable constraints
Risk
Cost
Performance
Sustainability
Constructability
Maintainability
Regulatory compliance
Confidence

Evaluation criteria SHALL be documented.

9. Decision Selection

Decision selection SHALL:

Compare all valid alternatives.
Justify the selected alternative.
Record rejected alternatives.
Preserve evaluation evidence.
Record decision rationale.

Selection SHALL be reproducible.

10. Decision Validation

Before release, Atlas SHALL verify:

Evidence sufficiency
Constraint compliance
Policy compliance
Traceability completeness
Explainability completeness
Confidence acceptability

Decisions failing validation SHALL NOT be communicated as final recommendations.

11. Decision Approval

Decision approval MAY be:

Automatic

For predefined low-risk scenarios.

Human-Assisted

Atlas recommends; humans approve.

Human-Required

Mandatory approval before execution.

Approval requirements SHALL be policy-driven.

12. Decision Outputs

Atlas MAY produce:

Engineering recommendations
Design decisions
Optimization proposals
Compliance reports
Risk assessments
Planning recommendations
Alternative rankings
Decision Support Artifacts

Every output SHALL include supporting rationale.

13. Decision Metadata

Each decision SHALL record:

Decision ID
Timestamp
Request source
Objectives
Constraints
Selected alternative
Rejected alternatives
Confidence
Risk assessment
Approval status
Decision rationale
Traceability references
Version
14. Decision Quality Attributes

Every engineering decision SHALL be evaluated for:

Correctness
Completeness
Consistency
Explainability
Traceability
Confidence
Robustness
Repeatability

Quality assessments SHALL be preserved.

15. Decision Monitoring

After communication, Atlas MAY monitor:

Decision outcomes
Implementation status
Performance metrics
Unexpected consequences
New evidence
Constraint changes

Significant changes MAY trigger a new decision cycle.

16. Conformance

An implementation conforms if it:

Uses canonical decision inputs.
Evaluates alternatives systematically.
Produces explainable decisions.
Maintains decision traceability.
Preserves governance requirements.
17. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Multi-objective decision optimization.
Collaborative decision-making.
Distributed decision engines.
Adaptive decision policies.
Autonomous decision refinement.

Such extensions SHALL remain consistent with the canonical Decision Architecture.